{"type":"message","id":"4163aa7a","parentId":"fdabbcea","timestamp":"2026-09-24T06:30:33.470Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<attachment>\nNo, it's not working. It went down a rabbit hole and is now trying to edit its own session transcript.\n\nWhat went wrong\n\nLook at the last thing it did: it found /bin/tau inside\n\n```\n/home/toxic/.tau/sessions/-/2026-09-24T06-06-11-238Z_...jsonl\n```\n\nand classified that as a STALE dependent. That file is the transcript of the conversation you're having with Solar 4 right now. The string /bin/tau appears in it because Solar 4 has been talking about /bin/tau for the last 15 minutes. It's not a dependent. It's a recording.\n\nThe discovery phase was too broad. Its rg command scanned ~/.tau wholesale, which includes:\n\n· ~/.tau/sessions/ — every past conversation, every tool call output\n· ~/.tau/logs/ — runtime logs\n· ~/.tau/cache/ — model caches\n\nEvery one of those matched the pattern dist/omp|/bin/tau|cli.ts because those strings are in the logs of every session where anyone discussed tau. So the agent got ~700 \"dependents,\" most of which are transcripts of prior conversations.\n\nThen it started fixing them. It rewrote the string /bin/tau inside session JSONL files. Those files are immutable logs — the tau system reads them to reconstruct history. Modifying them corrupts the session store. And when it modified the current session's log, it created a feedback loop: the fix itself became a new line in the log, which the next scan picked up as a new dependent.\n\nThat's why the tail shows it now searching for /bin/tau inside 2026-09-24T06-06-11-238Z.jsonl — that's the transcript of this exact session, and it's hunting for its own mention of the string it's been trying to fix.\n\nWhat actually got done\n\nBefore it got lost, some real work happened:\n\n· 262 stale references in actual scripts were rewritten: tau-audit.sh, tau-better, tau-direct, tau-fast, tau.sh, launcher scripts, nix package configs. Those fixes are fine.\n· 3 symlinks (omp, launcher/tau, frida-compile) were replaced with real copies. Those fixes are fine.\n· But it also corrupted the session store — probably dozens of session JSONL files got rewritten.\n\nImmediate damage control\n\nStop the agent. Kill the loop.\n\nThen check what it did to the session store:\n\n```bash\n# Count how many session files it modified\nfind ~/.tau/sessions -name '*.jsonl' -newermt '15 minutes ago' | wc -l\n\n# Check if any were actually modified (not just touched)\nfind ~/.tau/sessions -name '*.jsonl' -newermt '15 minutes ago' \\\n  | head -5 \\\n  | while read f; do\n      echo \"=== $f ===\"\n      grep -c '/home/toxic/.local/bin/tau' \"$f\" 2>/dev/null\n      grep -c '/bin/tau' \"$f\" 2>/dev/null\n    done\n```\n\nIf session files contain /home/toxic/.local/bin/tau where the original had /bin/tau, those transcripts are now altered and their replay history is inconsistent. Tau's session manager will either error or silently misread them.\n\nYou have a pre-merge tarball from earlier (~/.tau-premerge-*.tar.zst) but that predates a lot of today's work. The cleanest recovery is:\n\n1. Stop the agent now (Ctrl+C).\n2. Verify what actually needs fixing — the real dependents are scripts, launchers, and configs. Not logs. Not sessions. Not caches.\n3. Run the fix prompt below, which explicitly excludes runtime state directories from discovery.\n\nThe corrective prompt\n\n```text\n# TASK: Fix real dependents of tau — scripts, launchers, configs only\n\nThe prior run corrupted the session store by treating ~/.tau/sessions/*.jsonl\nas dependents and rewriting strings inside them. Those files are immutable\ntranscripts. Do not touch them. Do not scan them. Do not classify them.\n\nYour scope is NARROW: only files that are executables, scripts, config\nmanifests, package manifests, nix/docker files, or SKILL.md docs. Nothing\nunder any of these directories, ever:\n\n  ~/.tau/sessions/\n  ~/.tau/logs/\n  ~/.tau/cache/\n  ~/.tau/db/\n  ~/.tau/run/\n  ~/.tau/*.db\n  ~/.tau/*.db-shm\n  ~/.tau/*.db-wal\n  ~/sovereign/scratch/\n  any path containing /node_modules/ or /.git/ or /.bun/\n\n# PHASE 0 — Verify what the prior run did\n\n  # Count session files modified in the last hour\n  find ~/.tau/sessions -name '*.jsonl' -newermt '1 hour ago' | wc -l\n\n  # Show the first 3 filenames so we know the scope\n  find ~/.tau/sessions -name '*.jsonl' -newermt '1 hour ago' | head -3\n\n  # Show what changed inside one of them (should show binary diff or line\n  # replacement if the prior run altered them)\n  F=$(find ~/.tau/sessions -name '*.jsonl' -newermt '1 hour ago' | head -1)\n  grep -c 'HOME/.local/bin/tau' \"$F\" 2>/dev/null\n  grep -c '/bin/tau' \"$F\" 2>/dev/null\n\nReport the count and both grep counts. If the HOME-prefixed count is\nnonzero, the prior run corrupted those files. Say so and continue.\n\n# PHASE 1 — Restore corrupted session files if backup exists\n\n  # Look for the pre-merge tarball\n  ls -la ~/.tau-premerge-*.tar.zst 2>/dev/null\n\nIf a tarball exists, DO NOT extract it wholesale. Instead, for each\nsession JSONL modified in the last hour:\n\n  1. Extract only that file from the tarball to a temp location\n  2. Compare with the current file\n  3. If the tarball version differs from current, restore the tarball\n     version\n\n  # Example for one file\n  F=~/.tau/sessions/-/2026-09-24T06-06-11-238Z_*.jsonl\n  REL=$(echo \"$F\" | sed 's|^/home/toxic/||')\n  TARBALL=$(ls -t ~/.tau-premerge-*.tar.zst | head -1)\n  tar --zstd -xf \"$TARBALL\" -C /tmp \"$REL\" 2>/dev/null\n  # /tmp/$REL now holds the original\n  # cmp and restore if different\n\nIf no tarball exists, say so. Do not attempt to reconstruct session files\nby hand.\n\n# PHASE 2 — Discovery, restricted scope\n\n  rg -l --hidden \\\n     --glob '!**/sessions/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/logs/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/cache/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/node_modules/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/.git/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/.bun/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/scratch/**' \\\n     --glob '!**/*.db*' \\\n     --glob '!**/*.jsonl' \\\n     'dist/omp\\b|/bin/tau\\b|/bin/omp\\b|PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR|PI_CONFIG_DIR' \\\n     ~/sovereign ~/.config ~/.local/bin ~/.tau/extensions \\\n     2>/dev/null | sort -u > /tmp/deps-real.txt\n\n  wc -l /tmp/deps-real.txt\n\nReport the count. This is your real work list. It will be far smaller\nthan the ~700 from the prior run because session logs are excluded.\n\n  # Also grab launchers, even if the string isn't in them\n  find ~/sovereign/projects/tau/launcher ~/.local/bin \\\n       -maxdepth 1 -type f \\( -name '*tau*' -o -name '*omp*' \\) \\\n       2>/dev/null >> /tmp/deps-real.txt\n  sort -u /tmp/deps-real.txt -o /tmp/deps-real.txt\n  wc -l /tmp/deps-real.txt\n\n# PHASE 3 — Classify\n\nFor each file in /tmp/deps-real.txt:\n\n  - If it is a symlink, classify as ALIASED (and note target).\n  - If it is a regular file with link count > 1, classify as ALIASED_HARDLINK.\n  - If it contains the exact pattern (with word boundaries)\n    '(^|[^a-zA-Z0-9_/])/bin/tau([^a-zA-Z0-9_/]|$)' or the equivalents for\n    /bin/omp or dist/omp, and the referenced path does not exist,\n    classify as STALE.\n  - If it references the canonical tau path\n    /home/toxic/.local/bin/tau, classify as OK.\n  - Else, classify as OK_NO_REFERENCE.\n\nOutput a table:\n\n  | # | path | status | reason |\n\n# PHASE 4 — Fix STALE and ALIASED only\n\n  STALE: rewrite the exact string with word-boundary-safe sed:\n    sed -i -E 's:(^|[^a-zA-Z0-9_/])/bin/tau([^a-zA-Z0-9_/]|$):\\1/home/toxic/.local/bin/tau\\2:g' FILE\n    (and same for /bin/omp, dist/omp -> dist/tau)\n    Skip any file whose `file` output is not \"text\".\n    Skip any file matching *.db, *.db-shm, *.db-wal, *.jsonl.\n    Verify with the same word-boundary pattern after the fix.\n\n  ALIASED: replace the symlink with a copy of the target.\n    target=$(readlink -f \"$file\")\n    cp -a --no-preserve=links \"$target\" \"$file.tmp\"\n    mv \"$file.tmp\" \"$file\"\n    Verify it is no longer a symlink.\n\n  Do NOT touch session files, log files, or caches — they are excluded\n  from /tmp/deps-real.txt by construction, and you should recheck the\n  path against the exclusion list before writing anything.\n\n# PHASE 5 — Fork integrity\n\n  # Verify engine and vendor don't share inodes\n  find ~/sovereign/engine/packages -type f -links +1 | wc -l   # must be 0\n  find ~/sovereign/projects/tau/vendor/oh-my-pi/packages -type f -links +1 | wc -l\n\n  # Fork each hardlinked package as a real copy\n  for pkg in coding-agent agent ai tui utils catalog metaharness \\\n             snapcompact mnemopi wire omptype natives collab-web \\\n             browser-relay stats; do\n    src=~/sovereign/projects/tau/vendor/oh-my-pi/packages/$pkg\n    dst=~/sovereign/engine/packages/$pkg\n    [ -d \"$src\" ] || continue\n    [ -d \"$dst\" ] && [ \"$(stat -c '%i' \"$dst\" 2>/dev/null)\" = \\\n        \"$(stat -c '%i' \"$src\" 2>/dev/null)\" ] && {\n      rm -rf \"$dst\"\n      cp -a --no-preserve=links \"$src\" \"$dst\"\n    }\n  done\n\n  # Verify again\n  find ~/sovereign/engine/packages -type f -links +1 | wc -l   # must be 0\n\n# PHASE 6 — Report\n\nPrint:\n\n  - Session files modified by prior run: <count>\n  - Session files restored: <count>\n  - Real dependents found: <count>\n  - STALE fixed: <count>\n  - ALIASED fixed: <count>\n  - Forked packages: <list>\n  - Any UNRESOLVED entries\n\n# RULES\n\n- Never scan, read, classify, or write anything under ~/.tau/sessions/,\n  ~/.tau/logs/, ~/.tau/cache/, ~/.tau/db/, ~/.tau/run/, or any *.db,\n  *.db-shm, *.db-wal, *.jsonl file under ~/.tau/.\n- Use `file` to confirm a target is text before sed.\n- Word-boundary sed only. Never `s|/bin/tau|...|g` without the boundary\n  guard — that is what corrupted the current session's transcript in\n  the prior run.\n- Do not proceed to Phase 5 until Phase 4 reports zero UNRESOLVED.\n- Do not commit. Report and stop.\n```\n\nWhat I'd do right now\n\n1. Ctrl+C on the current session. The agent is scanning its own transcript and will keep finding itself.\n2. Run the verification commands in PHASE 0 of the corrective prompt manually first, so you know how much of the session store got rewritten.\n3. If the pre-merge tarball exists and covers today's session files, restore just the session files from it. If not, accept that today's transcripts are corrupted and move on — new sessions will be written correctly.\n\nThe real work — the launcher scripts, the nix config, the omp symlink — got fixed. The damage is to the transcript store, which is annoying but not fatal. The agent just needs narrower scope to finish.\n</attachment>"}],"steering":true,"attribution":"user","timestamp":1790231433030}}
